From fe5705360611dcb59b3e04187262efebb91bfda6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ning Wang <unidentifiedme26@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 11:24:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(caches): discover channels via _cat/aliases, pick max
 generation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`ChannelCache._discover_available_channels` previously probed a
hardcoded `generations = [43, 44, 45, 46]` × `versions = [...]`
cartesian product. Two failure modes followed:

1. Bit-rot: once Hydra publishes `latest-47-nixos-*` or beyond
   (currently gens 47 and 48 are live on search.nixos.org), the
   probe loop never sees them. They stay invisible until someone
   edits the list and cuts a release.

2. "First wins" picked the wrong generation: `_resolve_channels`
   iterated `available` and broke on the first `"unstable"` match.
   Because `available` was populated in ascending generation order,
   `unstable` resolved to the *lowest* live generation. With gens 45
   and 46 both live, the resolver picked gen 45 — two channel rolls
   behind what the search.nixos.org web UI returns for the same
   channel.

End-user symptom: `nix info` for a package on `unstable` returned a
stale version (e.g. aiohttp 3.13.3) while the web UI showed 3.13.5
from gen 48. The release channels were less affected because
`_resolve_channels` already sorted stable candidates by version, but
they were still capped by the hardcoded generation ceiling.

Replace the probe loop with a single `_cat/aliases?format=json` call
against the same Elasticsearch backend the project already uses,
filter via a regex on `latest-<gen>-nixos-<channel>` aliases, and
keep the existing per-alias `_count` probe so empty/zero-count
indices are still excluded. Refactor `_resolve_channels` to bucket
candidates per channel and pick the maximum generation for every
channel (not just stable releases), making the unstable and release
paths symmetric.

Also update `.github/copilot-instructions.md` so the description of
the channel-resolution system matches the new mechanism.

Existing `test_discover_channels` updated to mock the new
`_cat/aliases` + `_count` call sequence; new tests cover:
  - skipping aliases that report count=0
  - returning {} on a non-200 from _cat/aliases (so callers fall
    back to FALLBACK_CHANNELS)
  - picking the highest generation per channel for unstable
  - picking the highest generation per release version
  - picking the highest release version for `stable`/`beta`
  - resolving correctly when only `unstable` is live
